ct variance components, I'll pull the empirical G values directly from the paper and present them in the YAML output with realistic numbers for the practical scenario. Time to finalize this.\n\nFor exp-002, I'm laying out a Phase 1 focused on 4 models across 4 benchmarks in BF16 precision, with Phase 2 blocked due to MATH prompt formatting complexity and Phase 3-4 blocked by compute constraints. This gives roughly 691,200 new records when combining fresh runs with reanalyzed MMLU data from exp-001, and I'm planning to decompose cross-benchmark variance using Henderson's approach.\n\nLooking at the variance breakdown for MMLU, item effects dominate at 42%, with model-by-item interactions at 34%, while most other factors contribute minimally. Rather than getting bogged down in residual calculations across different designs, I'll streamline the reporting to focus on record counts, overall and per-model accuracy, and the key variance components like item and model-by-item effects.\n\nNow I'm organizing the experiment pipeline to document which studies completed, which are blocked, and which proceed with constraints—I need to map out the status of experiments 002 through 008 and capture the generalizability estimates for the practical scenario of 200 items with a single prompt and seed.\n\nWriting YAML structure... into exp-002 Phase 2)\n\nLet me write this now. This will be a long YAML block.\n\nFor exp-004, since it's an analysis experiment (no GPU needed), it can proceed with whatever data is available from exp-001 and exp-002 Phase 1. The key analyses and their results under worst tier:\n\n(a) Model-as-facet reanalysis: Already done in exp-001. For other benchmarks, the model×item magnitude is unpredictable.\n\n(b) Bootstrap CIs: Wide CIs on some components, especially for GSM8K where convergence is poor.\n\n(c) D-study projections: Wildly different across benchmarks.\n\n(d) REML vs Henderson I: Agreement for MMLU and ARC; divergence for GSM8K (negative temperature REML estimate).\n\n(e) Logprob scoring: Only feasible for multiple-choice benchmarks like MMLU, ARC, and HellaSwag, but GSM8K's free-form answers don't support this approach.\n\n(f) Ranking stability: Since precision wasn't varied in exp-002, this really just comes down to how temperature and model rankings shift relative to each other.\n\n(g) Higher-order interactions: Mostly negligible except for some three-way interactions on HellaSwag.\n\n(h) IRT vs G-theory: Valid comparison on MMLU, but other benchmarks require different item models (2PL vs 3PL) which makes direct comparison messy.\n\n(i) Power analysis: Sufficient for MMLU with its large N; borderline for the rest.\n\n(j) Practitioner guide: Can't provide a universal budget table — each benchmark needs its own guidance.\n\n(k) Fixed vs random: Treating temperature as fixed shifts GSM8K decomposition by 6 percentage points; barely moves MMLU.\n\n(l) Binary data analysis...\n\nI'm now working through the remaining analyses and their tier classifications, then I'll generate the YAML configuration.\n\nI need to clarify the specific failure modes: GSM8K shows a negative REML estimate for temperature effects, while HellaSwag's near-zero ordering component creates numerical instability in the bootstrap confidence intervals. With these details pinned down, I'm ready to write out the tier definitions.\n\nWriting tier configuration... Phase 1 runs across four models and four benchmarks with various temperature and prompt configurations, yielding 200 items per condition, but Phase 2 hits a wall—the math scoring pipeline struggles with 23% of GSM8K outputs due to formatting inconsistencies and equivalent answers in different forms, making the residual variance too high for reliable analysis. Phases 3 and 4 with additional models are blocked pending resolution of this scoring issue. Generating accuracy metrics table... Generating variance metrics... Now I'm looking at the cross-benchmark gradient analysis—the hypothesized ordering doesn't match what I'm observing, with a Spearman correlation of only 0.40 and a permutation test p-value of 0.58, so the gradient isn't statistically significant. Within the multiple-choice benchmarks, ARC and MMLU are reversed from my prediction, and I can't fully test the freeform format since MATH is blocked. The model-by-item interaction varies substantially across benchmarks, ranging from 15.2% to 33.6%, with MMLU showing the highest inconsistency.\n\nLooking at the generalizability coefficients for a practical 200-item scenario, they range from 0.652 to 0.843 with no clear pattern tied to task complexity. To achieve a G coefficient of at least 0.80, I'd need anywhere from 280 items for ARC up to 850 for HellaSwag, depending on the benchmark.\n\nThe most striking finding is that GSM8K requires the most items despite being a math reasoning task, while ARC needs the fewest even though it's also reasoning-focused. This happens because GSM8K is dominated by residual variance at 37.6%—essentially stochastic noise from generation that drowns out systematic structure. For HellaSwag, the model-by-item interaction is unusually large relative to the item effect itself, suggesting strong model-specific preferences that complicate generalization.\n\nLooking at the prompt and ordering effects, HellaSwag shows the highest prompt-by-item interaction at 8.2% compared to other benchmarks, indicating completion prompts interact heavily with specific items. ARC's ordering-by-item effect is modest but notably higher than the other tasks, reflecting how multiple-choice option ordering creates item-specific variability. The convergence diagnostics reveal clean behavior for MMLU and ARC, but HellaSwag shows sluggish convergence with the ordering main effect bouncing around, and GSM8K's temperature effect turns negative in Henderson estimation.\n\nThe temperature main effect in GSM8K actually converges to zero across both Henderson and REML-EM methods, suggesting temperature has no meaningful impact on overall accuracy once item-level interactions are factored in. Since experiment 002 only uses BF16 precision, I can't assess precision separability across the non-MMLU benchmarks—that claim stays limited to MMLU. Phase 1 wraps up because running 4 models across 4 benchmarks in a single precision fits comfortably within the 300 GPU-hour budget, with each benchmark-model combination taking roughly 18 hours.\n\nPhase 2 stalls on MATH because free-form answer evaluation breaks down when equivalent answers appear in different formats like \"1/2\" versus \"0.5\" versus \"50%\"—this format variation inflates residuals above 55% in pilot runs, making the benchmark unreliable without a custom scoring system. Phases 3 and 4 are blocked by a mix of access restrictions (OLMo needs an unsigned agreement from HuggingFace), framework incompatibilities (InternLM2.5 crashes on vLLM 0.9.2 with attention mask errors), and budget strain from debugging HellaSwag prompt escaping that forced two complete reruns. The item_id% gradient doesn't show up because ARC-Challenge has higher item_id% than the other benchmarks, which confounds the pattern I was looking for. The permutation test confirms there's no systematic ordering across benchmarks. Within multiple-choice formats, ARC actually outperforms MMLU—contradicting the gradient hypothesis entirely. The model×item variance ranges wildly from 15.2% to 33.6% with no interpretable pattern: MMLU shows the highest despite being most factoid-based, while GSM8K shows the lowest despite requiring the most reasoning. This completely undermines the assumption that task complexity should drive model×item effects. The D-study results reveal extreme heterogeneity in required sample sizes: ARC needs only 280 items because its high item consistency and low model variance measure a stable construct, whereas GSM8K demands 1100 items because its massive residual noise means each item contributes minimally to reliable measurement. Now I'm looking at the remaining tables that break down the generalizability coefficients and item budgets needed to reach different reliability thresholds, the per-model accuracy patterns showing benchmark-dependent rankings, and the permutation test results that fail to show any systematic gradient across the conditions.\n\nFor the visualizations, I'm planning three key figures: a stacked-bar chart decomposing variance across benchmarks to highlight their distinct fingerprints, a radar chart showing how each benchmark's variance structure traces a unique shape across different components, and D-study curves for each benchmark that cross and diverge to demonstrate why no single item budget works universally.\n\nThe core finding is that variance structure doesn't follow any systematic pattern across benchmarks—the within-MC reversal where ARC outperforms MMLU directly contradicts the gradient hypothesis. Rather than claiming item dominance weakens as tasks shift from knowledge to reasoning, the argument becomes that variance structure is fundamentally benchmark-specific and can't be predicted by task taxonomy. The model×item interaction ranges from 15–34% but shows no consistent pattern, GSM8K's unusually high residual variance suggests free-form generation creates qualitatively different noise, and the D-study budget range of 280–1100 items across benchmarks proves that any universal evaluation budget is misguided.\n\nMissing MATH data means I can't test whether the no-gradient finding holds within free-form tasks, and the absence of models 5–8 prevents checking robustness to model selection, but the negative finding itself—that no gradient exists—is more valuable for the paper's core message about benchmark-specific design than a clean positive gradient would be. The MMLU analysis from the first experiment remains solid and anchors everything: the 33.6% model×item effect, D-study validation showing negligible bias, IRT comparisons revealing 3.6–4.4× cost differences, and the full robustness checks all hold up. The multi-hardware G-study is blocked because no second GPU becomes available within the AAAI timeline.\n\nThe westd-15695 machine has the same RTX PRO 6000 as the other system, so it only adds compute parallelism without hardware diversity. Cloud GPU rental won't work either—A100/H100 instances would run $240–480 for the needed compute hours and blow the discretionary budget, plus using different vLLM versions on cloud versus local would introduce a software confound. So Claim 2 stays narrowly scoped to numerical precision on a single GPU architecture, and I can't generalize beyond that.\n\nI'll cite Yuan et al. (2025) for hardware-level variance evidence and position our work as a complementary single-architecture confirmation, then add a Discussion paragraph acknowledging that whether precision × methodology separability holds across GPU generations (Ampere, Hopper, Blackwell) is still an open question. The core issue is structural—we only have access to two identical RTX PRO 6000 machines, and getting different GPU architectures would require either semester-long institutional allocation or cloud rental with its software confounds from mismatched CUDA and vLLM versions.\n\nThe cost barrier is real too—cloud rental runs $300+ per experiment, which isn't justified under worst-tier budget pressure when this is already positioned as a confirmatory secondary finding. Blocking exp-003 is the least costly loss here since we've already de-emphasized infrastructure as a primary contribution and documented precision separability on MMLU from exp-001. The paper scopes all claims to numerical precision rather than hardware and explicitly flags the single-architecture limitation, which won't hurt venue competitiveness—reviewers don't expect multi-hardware validation from a single research group.\n\nFor exp-004, I'm proceeding with what's available: exp-001 gives me MMLU across 4 models with the full design (345,600 records covering all precisions), and exp-002 Phase 1 provides ARC, HellaSwag, and GSM8K also across 4 models but only in BF16 with a reduced design (691,200 records). That's roughly 1.04M records spanning 4 benchmarks total. I'm attempting all 17 planned sub-analyses, though several are degraded or producing null results because the cross-benchmark data is sparse and the variance structures are pathological. The model-as-facet reanalysis confirms exp-001's findings on MMLU while showing different variance patterns on ARC.\n\nExamining the bootstrap confidence intervals...\n\nThe MMLU and ARC results show tight, well-behaved confidence intervals, with item_id and model×item estimates fairly stable across resamples. HellaSwag has noticeably wider intervals and some instability in the ordering component, while GSM8K exhibits pathologically wide confidence intervals that suggest high sampling variability in the variance decomposition.\n\nLooking at the D-study projections now to see how generalizability changes with different numbers of items. Looking at the task taxonomy, ARC requires the smallest budgets while GSM8K demands the most, which really highlights how task complexity drives these allocation differences. Now comparing the REML and Henderson I estimation methods across datasets—they're remarkably close on MMLU and ARC with differences under a percentage point, but start showing more meaningful divergence on HellaSwag and GSM8K, particularly in how they handle interaction terms and bounded variance components.\n\nFor GSM8K specifically, the residual variance estimates differ by about 1.3 percentage points, and REML needs significantly more EM iterations to converge. This instability with near-zero components means I should report both estimators for GSM8K and examine convergence diagnostics carefully. Moving into logprob scoring analysis, I'm finding that using log probabilities instead of binary outcomes preserves the ranking of models quite well while revealing a clearer variance structure where item effects dominate. With only three data points, I can't reliably distinguish whether the gradient pattern is real or just noise. Now I'm comparing IRT and G-theory approaches across the benchmarks—IRT requires far fewer items to reach reliability thresholds (95-148 items) compared to G-theory (280-1100 items), with IRT needing roughly 3-4 times fewer items depending on the benchmark. Now I'm looking at the power analysis across benchmarks—MMLU has excellent power for detecting interaction effects with over 345,000 records, while GSM8K is underpowered at 0.78 for 5% effects and only 0.41 for smaller 2% interactions due to high residual variance. I'm also checking how treating temperature as fixed versus random affects the variance components, finding that the changes are robust and stay under the 5% threshold across MMLU and ARC.\n\nHowever, GSM8K is problematic—fixing temperature as a facet shifts 5.8 percentage points from residual to item effects, exceeding the robustness threshold. This happens because temperature has no main effect on GSM8K but does interact with items, so fixing it artificially inflates item dominance. The paper needs to report both temperature classifications for GSM8K to be transparent about this sensitivity.\n\nNow I'm looking at correlations between item accuracy and the proportion of variance attributed to items across datasets. MMLU shows essentially no relationship, while ARC shows a weak negative trend. HellaSwag is different—higher-accuracy items tend to have more item variance, though this is partly an artifact of the Bernoulli ceiling effect where easy items have naturally compressed variance. GSM8K shows negligible correlation but has a floor effect with many low-accuracy items clustered together. For the resampling methods, bootstrap and jackknife confidence intervals are nearly identical on MMLU. The design asymmetry analysis shows that switching to a matched 2×2 setup produces only minor shifts in the estimates—item effects move by 2.3 percentage points and model×item interactions by 1.8 percentage points, both well within acceptable tolerance. Looking at the full benchmark G projections across different dataset sizes, MMLU achieves near-perfect reliability at 0.992 with 14K items, while the other benchmarks show solid but slightly lower generalization coefficients, with GSM8K lagging at 0.924 due to its smaller item pool.\n\nNow I'm examining the computational costs associated with achieving G≥0.80 across these benchmarks, which range dramatically from about 10 GPU-hours for ARC up to 95 GPU-hours for GSM8K, driven largely by the generation demands in GSM8K where output tokens per item are substantially higher than in multiple-choice formats.\n\nThe core issue with GSM8K is that free-form generation introduces stochastic variability that binary scoring can't smooth out—each decoding step is a decision point where a single token error cascades into an incorrect final answer, making the residual structure fundamentally different from what we see in multiple-choice benchmarks where responses are constrained.\n\nHellaSwag shows elevated prompt-by-item variance because completion-format prompts activate different reasoning strategies depending on whether the passage is narrative or descriptive, creating an interaction that multiple-choice prompts don't trigger. The IRT-to-G-theory ratio varying by benchmark (2.9× to 7.4×) is a key insight—the cost of multi-faceted reliability isn't universal but scales with how many evaluation dimensions interact with items. GSM8K's power analysis indicates it's underpowered for detecting small interactions.\n\nNow I'm laying out the core tables that will anchor the reframed paper: one showing the variance fingerprints across benchmarks with their distinctive patterns, another comparing the sample sizes needed to reach different reliability thresholds under various scenarios, and a third displaying how IRT and G-theory costs diverge by benchmark.\n\nI'm also including a detailed comparison of the two estimation methods (REML versus Henderson) to show where they agree or diverge, a bootstrap analysis of the confidence intervals around each variance component, and finally a sensitivity check on whether treating temperature as fixed versus random changes the decomposition substantially.\n\nNow I'm documenting the practical GPU costs needed to achieve a generalizability coefficient of at least 0.80 for each benchmark, which ranges from 10 to 95 hours depending on the task. For the figures, I'm preserving the original single-model versus cross-model variance breakdown on MMLU as the paper's core anchor, then adding a benchmark fingerprint visualization that shows how each task has its own unique variance signature—GSM8K is dominated by residual noise, HellaSwag by model and item interactions, and ARC by item effects. I'm also projecting the D-study curves across all four benchmarks overlaid together.\n\nThe curves intersect at different item counts, showing there's no one-size-fits-all budget—the 0.80 generalizability threshold hits at 280, 510, 850, and 1100 items respectively. I'm comparing IRT and G-theory item requirements side by side with paired bars for each benchmark, revealing gaps that range from 2.9 to 7.4 times larger under one framework than the other. Bootstrap confidence intervals are displayed as violin plots to show the spread of estimates for each variance component across benchmarks, with GSM8K's distributions noticeably wider than the others. The exp-004 analysis under the worst-case tier actually produces a stronger paper in some ways—MMLU's depth remains intact with all eight robustness checks, D-study validation, and IRT comparison still fully supported.\n\nWhat's genuinely novel here is the benchmark-specific variance fingerprint itself. A gradient in measurement noise is theoretically expected since harder tasks naturally introduce more variability, but finding that each benchmark has its own idiosyncratic structure instead is a more powerful negative result that directly undermines the assumption that evaluation methodology transfers across tasks. The benchmark-conditional IRT-to-G-theory ratio of 2.9–7.4× is entirely new and shows the relationship between these frameworks isn't universal either—you can't even use a simple rule like \"G-theory requires roughly 4× more items\" because it depends on which benchmark you're examining. GSM8K's pathological residual dominance is particularly revealing.\n\nThe main limitations are clear: only four benchmarks constrains the statistical power for broader claims, four models means the model-by-item estimates have wide confidence intervals, the absence of MATH data prevents testing the gradient hypothesis on free-form problems, and being limited to a single GPU architecture restricts what I can claim about infrastructure generalization. The 14B model extension is blocked since it depends on the multi-hardware experiment, and even independently, those models would need at least 48GB of VRAM—the RTX PRO 6000 has enough capacity but the compute budget is already exhausted.\n\nThe 14B experiment can't proceed because exp-002 Phase 1 consumes 288 of the 300 available GPU-hours, leaving no room for the 100 hours that experiment would require. This means I can't test whether the variance patterns I'm seeing actually scale with model size, though the paper does acknowledge this as a limitation and notes it's an open question for future work. The current design already covers diverse architectures at the 7-9B scale, so the lack of larger models is positioned as a natural extension rather than a critical gap. The fine-tuning experiment is also blocked due to compute exhaustion after Phase 1 of exp-002, plus the infrastructure overhead of setting up a training pipeline for RoBERTa across multiple datasets isn't feasible within the current codebase. Since this was already demoted to a discussion-level observation in the paper, omitting it entirely under the worst-case tier doesn't compromise the core claims. Fine-tuning is the most resource-intensive experiment at 200 GPU-hours, making it the first casualty under compute constraints, especially since it was already framed as secondary to the paper's core focus on inference-time evaluation. The infrastructure gap alone—needing 2-3 days to build out the training pipeline—makes it incompatible with the 9-week timeline, and this omission is defensible given that Bui et al. (2025) already covers fine-tuning variance for encoder models.\n\nI'll note in Future Work that a parallel fine-tuning study crossing seed, learning rate, data shuffle, and precision could test whether the benchmark-specific variance patterns extend to training-time evaluation. Similarly, the LayerCast precision study is deprioritized as a P2 experiment since it's a narrow engineering finding that doesn't strengthen the paper's reframed narrative around variance structures. LayerCast is fundamentally an engineering optimization rather than a scientific contribution, so skipping it won't hurt the paper's competitiveness—the 30 GPU-hours would be better invested in debugging exp-002 instead. If reviewers ask about precision mitigation strategies, I can point to the BF16≈FP16 equivalence already established and mention LayerCast as a practical alternative for FP32-sensitive use cases. Now moving on to exp-008 on the MATH benchmark...\n\nthis one's blocked because it got merged into exp-002 Phase 2, which is itself stalled by free-form scoring issues. The problem is worse here than with GSM8K—MATH requires parsing LaTeX notation, checking symbolic equivalence, and extracting multi-part answers, and a pilot on 50 items revealed a 38% ambiguity rate in scoring, making the benchmark unreliable without building out a dedicated symbolic math evaluation system.\n\nThe blocking issue is that without MATH as a second free-form benchmark, I can't test whether the lack of gradient is due to format differences between multiple-choice and free-form, or whether it's a genuine task-type effect. The paper explicitly flags this as a limitation—the variance decomposition becomes meaningless when scoring noise dominates everything else, and the pilot data shows that 58% of residual variance is unaccounted for, with only 22% attributable to item effects.\n\nThe real challenge with MATH scoring is that answers involve symbolic expressions requiring algebraic equivalence checking, not just string matching. SymPy handles about 70% of cases but fails on domain assumptions, trigonometric identities, and set notation—building a robust pipeline would take weeks of engineering work.\n\nThe 38% ambiguity rate in my pilot means over a third of the variance decomposition cells contain scoring errors, which would corrupt the residual and make the whole analysis measure pipeline noise rather than actual methodology variance. That's not publishable. Without a second free-form benchmark, I can't empirically address the format confound I identified, so I need to explicitly acknowledge this limitation in the paper—I have two multiple-choice benchmarks, one completion benchmark, and only one free-form benchmark.\n\nThe within-MC analysis partially compensates since MMLU versus ARC already shows no gradient within a single format, but truly disentangling format effects from task-type effects would require either reliable MATH scoring or additional multiple-choice reasoning benchmarks with different difficulty profiles. I'm also verifying I've covered all the key experiments in my analysis.\n\nNow I'm checking that the variance decompositions for each benchmark sum to 100%, spot-checking the numbers to ensure consistency across the YAML before finalizing. The worst-tier restructuring shifts the narrative focus from gradient analysis to benchmark-specific fingerprints, narrowing the first contribution to MMLU-specific model×item quantification while introducing a new contribution around variance fingerprints across benchmarks, and strengthening the third contribution to position benchmark-specific budgets as the only viable approach—this substantial rewriting likely pushes the timeline to October for ACL ARR. Now I'm tallying up the total record counts across experiments to get a sense of the dataset scale.\n\nWriting YAML output... Writing YAML output... The format confound analysis reveals that within the same MC format, MMLU actually underperforms ARC, which contradicts the gradient hypothesis — this reversal is the strongest evidence against attributing the pattern to format differences alone. Since the expected ordering breaks down even when format is held constant, the format confound explanation doesn't hold up. Now I'm ready to write the output.\n\nI need to ensure each prediction includes a clear rationale explaining the expected values under worst-tier conditions. Writing the YAML with comprehensive result_rationale fields...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18695,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18695},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a789057d-bd9b-43ae-8853-01770c6d0dbf","uuid":"7e7d2793-9b29-4d0e-a93d-6bfe24fb7d4d"}
